PICONIX FD - EXACT KEY OPERATIONS (NOT LINUX fd FIND)

Start: terminal_send({"text":"fd /tmp"}) then terminal_send({"key":"ENTER"}); terminal_read({}).
fd is a full-screen file browser. It is NOT the Linux fd search tool.

DIRECTORY LIST:
  UP/DOWN                select entry; traverse batches of 48
  RIGHT / ENTER          open directory or read-only file viewer
  LEFT / BACKSPACE / BS  parent directory
  /                      filter file names (case-insensitive substring)
  ENTER                  confirm filter; ESC cancels input
  c                      clear name filter
  r                      reload directory
  e                      open selected editable file with edit (NOT vi)
  q                      quit fd

FILE VIEWER (/tmp /etc /proc /man text files only):
  UP/DOWN                scroll one line
  PgUp/PgDn              scroll page on a physical ANSI terminal
  /                      search within file, then ENTER
  n / N                  next / previous matching line
  r                      refresh viewer
  LEFT / q / ESC         return to directory list
  e                      launch edit for writable /tmp or /etc file

IMPORTANT: q in a viewer returns to list; q at list exits fd.
fd never previews /dev nodes and never edits /proc or /man.
fd does not copy, rename or delete files. For vi editing, close fd
and terminal_send({"text":"vi /tmp/NAME"}) followed by ENTER at the shell prompt.

MCP workflow: open fd -> read -> send key DOWN or RIGHT -> read
-> send key LEFT -> read -> send key q -> back to shell.
A successful terminal_send means keystrokes were delivered, NOT that
the intended screen was reached. Read screen after each navigation.
terminal_read always returns the current screen; if it looks unchanged,
do not assume the key worked. Verify the mode or another observable result.

MCP terminal_read embeds the cursor in the screen as ESC[4m...ESC[24m.
Read again after navigation: cursor movement changes the screen stream.
